1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
And now it's the old scout master.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:18,000
We're looking out over camp and in our part of the world recently the American Bald Eagle has returned

3
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:23,000
and now has a nesting site actually on the property of our camp.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:29,000
We feel very privileged and we were looking out and we saw an eagle soaring way up in the sky there-

5
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Just the most beautiful, majestic sight, as you would expect.

6
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,000
And also off we saw a few hawks flying by

7
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:46,000
And I asked one of the fellows with me if he knew an easy way to tell the difference between hawks and eagles

8
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:47,000
And he said no, I really don't.

9
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:51,000
I said: well, it's the wingtip feathers which are called pinions.

10
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:52,000
They're a good indicator.

11
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Eagles have five and hawks have four.

12
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,000
And some people say it's just a matter of opinion.

13
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Yeah, a matter of opinion.

14
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Oh my,

15
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Don't blame me for that one folks.

16
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:09,000
That's Tony Rotswell.

17
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Thank you, Tony.

18
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:12,000
He sent the story in.

19
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Hey, this is podcast number 73..

20
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

21
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,000
This is Clark Greene. heard from Larry this week who said:

22
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:50,000
I just wanted to say that the podcast and the interview was great.

23
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,000
That was last podcast. we interviewed the author of the Scoutmaster's Other Handbook

24
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,000
and the Eagle Court of Honor Handbook, Mark Ray,

25
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
who was generous enough to sit and talk with us for a bit.

26
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,000
I've listened to you interview several people and I have to compliment you on your interview style.

27
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,000
You've got a great delivery and a great program.

28
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:09,000
Well, thanks, Larry.

29
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:10,000
I sure appreciate that.

30
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,000
Other news this week: let's see. looking around,

31
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,000
It's June and so people are out doing what they should be doing.

32
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:18,000
They're outside,

33
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:19,000
They're camping,

34
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,000
They're off with their troops.

35
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Summer camp is right around the corner.

36
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:24,000
It's an exciting part of the year.

37
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,000
We've got big plans for our troop this year.

38
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:31,000
We're off for a week of summer camp here at the end of July and then we're off to Switzerland

39
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:36,000
to visit Kondersteg International Scout Center in Switzerland for a week.

40
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
So it's going to be a very exciting and very busy summer.

41
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
Over on Facebook this week, well, we hit a nice big milestone

42
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:47,000
because we got 1,000 fans on Facebook now.

43
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,000
So thanks very much, folks.

44
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:54,000
This time around on the Scoutmaster podcast, we're going to listen to a story.

45
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
This is a story of a scouting hero of mine, and I'm sure that most of you will share the same opinion-

46
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,000
and we have to tell these stories.

47
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:04,000
We have to keep this stuff in front of us.

48
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:09,000
It's part of our history as a movement, and I think it's inspiring to be able to go back

49
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:15,000
and to look at how things happened and what got us and who got us to where we are today.

50
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:18,000
So all the way, let's get started, shall we?

51
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:27,000
This has to be the truth, folks, because there is no way anyone could make this up.

52
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,000
The year is 1926, we're in New Jersey-

53
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Yeah, New Jersey- and we're in a warehouse

54
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:48,000
and a crate, a big heavy crate of World War I surplus semaphore flagpoles.

55
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:54,000
It sounds like a bad joke, I know, but this actually happened.

56
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:59,000
This big heavy crate shifted and fell

57
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:06,000
and because of that the whole face of worldwide scouting changed.

58
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:11,000
Now I'm a little ahead of the story, obviously

59
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Now understand why that crate is so important.

60
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:20,000
We've got to go back about 26 years and then we have to go a considerable distance

61
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,000
from that warehouse in New Jersey.

62
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:28,000
We've got to go all the way over the Atlantic to Ahu's, Denmark.

63
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:34,000
In August of 1900, a boy was born in the city of Ahu's,

64
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:39,000
named Wilhelm Biharagard Jensen.

65
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:47,000
Wilhelm grew up and became a boy who liked to go roaming through the woods outside Ahu's.

66
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:51,000
His older brother, Harold, sent him a Christmas present one year,

67
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:56,000
and that Christmas present was the book Baden Powell's Scouting for Boys.

68
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:00,000
The book had been a big smash success all over Europe

69
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:02,000
and had been translated into Danish.

70
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Well, Wil read the book and discovered that Scouts did the kind of things that he wanted to do

71
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:10,000
when he was out there in the woods: hiking and tracking and camping

72
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:14,000
and cooking over fires and all this great stuff.

73
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:19,000
And he also discovered that to become a Boy Scout you had to join a patrol.

74
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:23,000
Well, there was no patrol around, not that Wil knew about.

75
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:27,000
So he got his buddies together and he made up a patrol.

76
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:32,000
That patrol did the sort of adventurous stuff that he had hoped they would do.

77
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:37,000
They got out in the woods outside of Ahu's and went camping and hiking.

78
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:43,000
But they had meetings that kind of devolved into some kind of rough tumble games

79
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,000
that Wil didn't particularly care for.

80
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:51,000
And besides that, about 1912 there was a big downturn in the Danish economy

81
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:58,000
and Wil's family needed to move and follow his father's work from place to place for several years.

82
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Four years later Wil and his family were back in Ahu's.

83
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:06,000
Wil was a bit older and he formed his patrol. he was their patrol leader

84
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:09,000
and they just went scouting all the time.

85
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:16,000
They camped on an island on a nearby lake and they put together theatrical presentations

86
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
with other patrols to raise some money.

87
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:22,000
They tramped around the countryside, they hiked and camped one summer

88
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:26,000
all along the west coast of Denmark and Wil just flourished.

89
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:34,000
He attained the rank of night scout, which was the highest in Danish scouting in 1917.

90
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:38,000
His circle of influence grew. his patrol was now part of a real troop

91
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:44,000
and he was the most senior patrol leaders and often led the group as a whole.

92
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:47,000
He came into contact with other scout troops in the community

93
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:53,000
and began to see the wider circle of scouting as it formed in those early days.

94
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:59,000
By the time he was 20 years old, Wil was still involved with scouts who was helping lead his troop

95
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:02,000
and he was also studying to become a pharmacist.

96
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:09,000
But then he was selected to go to the First World Jamboree at Olympia in London

97
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:12,000
and that's where he first met Baden Powell.

98
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:16,000
Going to that First World Jamboree really spiked Wil's interest.

99
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:21,000
He began to get very serious about his leadership in scouting.

100
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:27,000
He became the scout master of the troupe in Ahus and a trainer in national training courses.

101
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:33,000
He began to write for the National Danish Scout Journal and then he actually became its editor.

102
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:36,000
His interest in journalism grew. he also wrote for newspapers

103
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:39,000
and he published a book when he was 23 years old.

104
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:45,000
It was a novel, it was called The Island and it was based on his experience with his patrol.

105
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:52,000
At the age of 25, Wil decided that he needed to see more of the world.

106
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:55,000
His interest was piqued by going to the World Jamboree.

107
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:58,000
He decided what he was going to do was travel around the world

108
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:04,000
and kind of investigate scouting in other countries and bring the best ideas back to Denmark.

109
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:09,000
So he started out in the United States and that year when he traveled to the United States

110
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:14,000
he got a job in a scout camp. and after the scout camp season was over

111
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:21,000
he managed to wrangle himself a job, a very menial job- in the offices of the Boy Scouts of America.

112
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:31,000
And that's where Wil came into contact with that famous crate of World War I, surplus semaphore flagpoles,

113
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:33,000
I mean, come on.

114
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:40,000
So he's moving the crate around, the crate slips and falls and it traps his leg, his right leg,

115
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:45,000
and it breaks his right leg. and so Wil is now in a foreign country somewhere,

116
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:51,000
but he's being well taken care of. he's got a busted leg. he ends up laid up for a week in the hospital

117
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:55,000
but, you know, pretty soon. he's on crutches and he's moving around.

118
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:58,000
So once Wil was out of the hospital he was back at the offices

119
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:02,000
and he had a chance encounter with the chief scout executive, James West.

120
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:06,000
Now, you know, the BSA was a relatively small operation at the time

121
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:12,000
and West knew who Wil was, at least by an accident report,

122
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:16,000
and saw him on the crutches there and he said: hey, how you doing?

123
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:20,000
Oh, I'm doing fine. What do you think of the Boy Scouts of America?

124
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:26,000
And Wil gave him a brief answer right there in person.

125
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:32,000
but he also decided that he really wanted to carefully answer West's question

126
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:38,000
because Wil was a serious man and he had made this work the aim of his life.

127
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:44,000
So he sat down and he composed an 18 page single spaced memo

128
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:48,000
and kind of dissected the BSA's strengths and weaknesses.

129
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:51,000
He had a lot of good things to say about American scouting,

130
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:57,000
but he wasn't too sure that there wasn't this kind of mania for collecting merit badges.

131
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:02,000
He thought the camps were a little soft, They were a little luxurious.

132
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:11,000
Most important, Wil said troops weren't really using the patrol method that Baden Powell had set up.

133
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:19,000
Well, West read the memo and discovered that he had a very articulate, well studied young man

134
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:27,000
working for him in a warehouse who really had a handle on some things about scouting.

135
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:34,000
So West persuaded him to become part of the BSA's editorial service.

136
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:39,000
One thing led to another. One of Wil's suggestions had been

137
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:43,000
that there really needed to be some kind of a guide for patrol leaders

138
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,000
if the patrol method was really to be applied.

139
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:49,000
and he was pretty insistent that it should be written by somebody

140
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:52,000
who had been a patrol leader or been a scout master.

141
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:55,000
and so West kind of called his bluff on that and said:

142
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:57,000
well, why don't you write it?

143
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
Well, that was all that Wil needed to hear,

144
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:02,000
and he started working hard putting things together.

145
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:06,000
Now I'm still calling him Wil, but by that time he was calling himself Bill,

146
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:10,000
and the reason that he did that he kind of Americanized his name.

147
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:16,000
He had Americanized his name and he changed from Wilhelm Beherrgaard Jensen

148
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,000
to Wilhelm Beherrgaard.

149
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:22,000
But he noticed that people at the BSA office had a little trouble with Beherrgaard

150
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:25,000
and it was coming a little bit out like Beergarden

151
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:28,000
and he didn't like the association.

152
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:33,000
So he freely translated his Danish name, Beherr, which means hill,

153
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:39,000
and Gard, which means court, to Hillcourt

154
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:43,000
and Wilhelm and William. that worked,

155
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:48,000
so he became known as William Hillcourt

156
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:50,000
and to his friends, Bill.

157
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:53,000
So now you know who Wil is right,

158
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:57,000
William Greenbar. Bill Hillcourt

159
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:01,000
the author of the first handbook for patrol leaders,

160
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:05,000
and it was published in 1929

161
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:08,000
and translated into five other languages

162
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:10,000
for use around the world.

163
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
While he was working at the BSA offices,

164
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
Bill met his wife, Grace Brown,

165
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
who worked on the chief executive staff,

166
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:19,000
and Grace was from Yonkers,

167
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:23,000
and by June of 1933

168
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:26,000
Grace Brown became Grace Hillcourt.

169
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:29,000
After a long honeymoon to Europe

170
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:33,000
and trekking around and cycling through Europe,

171
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
they returned back home

172
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:38,000
and Bill began writing and editing Scouting Magazine.

173
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:41,000
Once the handbook for patrol leaders was published,

174
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,000
Bill was pulled in on another job

175
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:46,000
and that was aimed at saving Boys Life Magazine.

176
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
It was the midst of the depression.

177
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
Boys Life was not doing well

178
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:53,000
and the Rockefeller Foundation made a small loan

179
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
to help keep Boys Life afloat,

180
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:59,000
and Bill's contribution was a single page

181
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
in each month's magazine

182
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
aimed at patrol leaders

183
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,000
and the members of their patrol

184
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:07,000
and all the great stuff patrols could do

185
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:10,000
and how they could advance together through Scouting,

186
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,000
all the great activities they could do.

187
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:16,000
and so the boy who had been a patrol leader in Ahus

188
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:20,000
now became America's patrol leader,

189
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:23,000
and Greenbar Bill was born

190
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
In the years that followed,

191
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:28,000
Bill's role expanded from being the BSA's chief journalist

192
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
on Scoutcraft and troop leadership

193
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:33,000
to becoming one of the primary program researchers

194
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:34,000
and developers,

195
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:37,000
and he did this in a really interesting way,

196
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
a very hands-on kind of guy.

197
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
He found a perfect location,

198
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:44,000
the village of Mendham, New Jersey,

199
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:48,000
just outside of the newly donated Shift Scout Reservation.

200
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:52,000
Bill and Grace talked Dr West

201
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:55,000
into allowing them to convert an old stone sheep barn

202
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,000
into a house for the two of them

203
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:00,000
where they could be close to the troop

204
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,000
that he was the scoutmaster for

205
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:04,000
Mendham New Jersey's troop. one

206
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:08,000
was the experimental troop of the BSA

207
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:13,000
In 1935, while they were there at Shift Scout Reservation,

208
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:17,000
Bill and Grace met Olive and Robert.

209
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:20,000
You know who Olive and Robert are- right,

210
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:21,000
Baden Powell.

211
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
That's a familiar name in Scouting,

212
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
the founder of Scouting.

213
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:28,000
Lord and Lady Baden Powell

214
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:30,000
were on a visit to the United States

215
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:32,000
and they were happy to meet

216
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,000
with people at Shift Scout Reservation

217
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:36,000
and the training center there

218
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:39,000
and they became a particularly fond of Bill and Grace

219
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:42,000
and it started a lifelong friendship

220
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:45,000
with Lord and Lady Baden Powell

221
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:48,000
and continued with their children and grandchildren.

222
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:50,000
Bill became one of the most familiar faces

223
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:53,000
and names in Scouting in the United States.

224
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:56,000
He wrote articles for Scouting magazine.

225
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:58,000
He wrote articles for Boys Life.

226
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:01,000
He wrote two editions of the Handbook for Scoutmasters,

227
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:03,000
two editions of the Boy Scout Handbook.

228
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:07,000
He wrote the first Scout Field Book,

229
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:10,000
which just became a classic text

230
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:13,000
on basic campcraft and nature lore.

231
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:16,000
He took wood badge training under John Skinner Wilson.

232
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:19,000
He was the camp chief of Gilwell Park in 1936.

233
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
And after World War II

234
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
Bill was instrumental in bringing

235
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
wood badge courses to the United States

236
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:28,000
and he became the first Gilwell Deputy Camp Chief

237
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,000
in the United States in 1948

238
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:33,000
and the first BSA Wood Badge Scoutmaster.

239
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:36,000
He edited Baden Powell's Scouting for Boys

240
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,000
and aided it to Scoutmastership

241
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:40,000
to be used to help revitalize Scouting

242
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,000
in war-torn countries,

243
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
and the books have been translated

244
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:46,000
into more than 20 languages.

245
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:49,000
He helped develop junior leader training courses

246
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:52,000
and new requirements for Boy Scout advancement.

247
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,000
He corresponded with Scouters in other countries,

248
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,000
devising them on training.

249
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:58,000
He met Scouters from around the nation

250
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:00,000
and the world at national and world jamborees

251
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:03,000
in which he was always in attendance.

252
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:05,000
Bill retired from professional service

253
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,000
with the BSA in 1965

254
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:10,000
and he was able to put into print

255
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,000
something that he had been working on

256
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
for a long time

257
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:17,000
with Grace and Lady: Baden Powell.

258
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:20,000
that was the first authoritative biography of Baden Powell,

259
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
published under the title Baden Powell:

260
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
Two Lives of a Hero.

261
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,000
It was translated into seven foreign languages

262
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,000
and became the standard reference work

263
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:31,000
on Baden Powell for many, many years.

264
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,000
Bill and Grace traveled widely.

265
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
They visited Scout friends

266
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:38,000
and helped to implement training programs

267
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
for Scout associations all over the world.

268
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:43,000
and Grace died in 1973,

269
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:46,000
leaving Bill bereft of his great friend

270
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
and companion.

271
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
but he pressed on with his volunteer work

272
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
in scouting.

273
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
In the 1970s the BSA made some changes.

274
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:57,000
Bill didn't like those changes at all.

275
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:00,000
He watched the BSA de-emphasize

276
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
the outdoor aspects of scouting

277
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
and try and make some pretty desperate moves

278
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
to remain relevant.

279
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
but in 1978

280
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
Bill convinced chief scout executive

281
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
Harvey Price

282
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
to allow him to write

283
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
a new Boy Scout Handbook

284
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
and bring back the outdoor orientation

285
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
to the Boy Scouts of America.

286
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:23,000
and Bill did this for free

287
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
and he spent a year

288
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
and brought out the ninth edition

289
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
entitled the official Boy Scouts Handbook,

290
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
and it was an immediate success

291
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
and it really did serve to revitalize scouting

292
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:36,000
again again.

293
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
This was his second go-around

294
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
at the age of 78

295
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
and his efforts were recognized

296
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
by the National Court of Honor

297
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
and he was awarded the silver buffalo

298
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
for distinguished service to American youth.

299
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
Bill spent his last years

300
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
as a worldwide goodwill ambassador

301
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
for scouting

302
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
and he tried to never miss a jamboree

303
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
or big encampment.

304
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
and even though it had been

305
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
some time since he had written a column

306
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,000
for Boy's Life magazine,

307
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:07,000
his signature was still highly sought after.

308
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
and that signature

309
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
was kind of interesting,

310
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
This Bill signature

311
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,000
superimposed on two green bars.

312
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:19,000
At the 1989 National Jamboree

313
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:21,000
people lined up for hours

314
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:25,000
and Bill signed 7,423 autographs

315
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:27,000
in the middle of

316
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:29,000
a hot Virginia summer

317
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:31,000
at the age of 89..

318
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:34,000
One of those 7,423 autographs

319
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
is right here

320
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
while I'm speaking to you,

321
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,000
a copy of the original

322
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
fieldbook that Bill wrote.

323
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
that I happen to have

324
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
and I brought to the jamboree with me

325
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
and I stood in line

326
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
and I got to meet the man for just a moment.

327
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
He was sitting at a table there

328
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
signing books very happily,

329
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
and when he saw that original copy

330
00:18:56,000 --> 00:19:00,000
of the fieldbook come across

331
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
he looked at it

332
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
and he looked up at me

333
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
and he looked at his assistant

334
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
and he turns a two-fer

335
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
And what he meant

336
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
when he said that was

337
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
that he would put both signatures.

338
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
he would put the Bill signature with the green bars

339
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
and his more formal signature in it.

340
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
and I have it and it's a treasured little artifact

341
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
that I have today

342
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
from that very brief meeting

343
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
with really one of Scouting's heroes.

344
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
It all started

345
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
with that big crate

346
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
of World War I surplus

347
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
7,4 flagpoles-

348
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
and it led to something

349
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
that changed Scouting

350
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
permanently

351
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
and to something that we've

352
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
still benefit from today:

353
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
the generous, generous heart

354
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
of a great man, William Greenbar

355
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
Bill Hillcourt.

356
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
He passed away on November 9

357
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
of 1992 while he was on a

358
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
Scouting tour. He was in Sweden.

359
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
he was very close to his home

360
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
and his boyhood home in Denmark,

361
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
but he passed away there

362
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
and was brought back

363
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
to New Jersey and has entered

364
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
next to his beloved grace there

365
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
in Cemetery in New Jersey

366
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
and somebody who's well remembered.

367
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
now, if you haven't read

368
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
the original

369
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
Patrol Eater's Handbook,

370
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
if you haven't read the old

371
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
fieldbook, the first edition of the fieldbook,

372
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
you know it's worth getting a copy,

373
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
it's worth looking at and you can

374
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
virtually shake hands

375
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
with Greenbar Bill Hillcourt

